well plant smuggling can be a prickly  business especially when your spoils are  cacti  but in all seriousness the theft of some  species from chile's atacama desert  represents an existential threat to the  plants  now authorities in italy and chile have  cracked down  a horde of poached cactus plants stolen  from the atacama desert in chile  some of these prickly plants are  centuries old  the total haul discovered by the police  a year ago since returned home  is valued at over 1.2 million dollars on  the black market  to make this happen police from chile  and italy have worked together with the  association for biodiversity and  conservation for more than a year  poaching plants is becoming a problem  and it's a crime that threatens  biodiversity  plants are not very often  linked to extinction risk like when you  think about a threatened species you  usually don't think about a plant  and i think part of it is because  plants are everywhere so we take them  for granted even though  many more plant species are threatened  with extinction than  vertebrates the phenomenon is known as  plant blindness  hundreds of cacti species are more  threatened with extinction than birds or  mammals and  most people are not aware of it often  smugglers ease their way through  customs without hassle because even some  customs officers are not aware of the  illegal plant trade  but this time it's different the  confiscation and subsequent repatriation  of the plants have made headlines around  the world  it's raised awareness something that may  help prevent future cac  napping
 

